=======================================================================
 test_triangulate_2d_polygon - the triangulate command (2D polygons)
=======================================================================

WHAT THIS IS

  Tests of LaGriT's

      triangulate / counterclockwise | clockwise

  which fills a polygon, given as an ordered list of vertices, with
  triangle cells by ear clipping (src/triangulate_lg.f).

  Every test checks that the result is a VALID triangulation of the
  polygon: n - 2 cells for n vertices, no zero-area cell, no inverted
  cell, cells exactly covering the polygon, every polygon edge used by
  one cell. The polygons concentrate on what broke the original code:
  many collinear vertices along a side, very dense boundaries, sharp
  vertices that come very close to a far part of the boundary, and
  coordinates far from the origin.

  Terms: a polygon has VERTICES; triangulate makes triangle CELLS.

QUICK START

  run_triangulate_tests.sh  [path-to-lagrit]        about 20 s
  BIG=1 run_triangulate_tests.sh  [path-to-lagrit]  also tri21, the
                                                    40 004-vertex
                                                    square: + ~34 s

  then open, in a web browser,

      logs/summary.html

  Requires: LaGriT, Python 3 (standard library only), a POSIX shell.

  WITH THE ORIGINAL triangulate_lg.f: 22 of the 41 decks FAIL. That is
  the bug report, in test form. WITH THE FIXED triangulate_lg.f (Oct
  2026): all PASS, and tri38 is REFUSED as intended. See THE BUGS.

  tri01 spells LaGriT in block letters, triangulates them, then
  refines and smooths them into a good mesh; tri02-13 are STRESS tests
  (combs, spirals, holes, slivers, starbursts); tri14-41 target the
  bugs. (Renumbered Oct 12 2026: the old tri41 is tri01, old tri29-40
  are tri02-13, old tri01-28 are tri14-41.)

