  (1) "ERROR: Unable to triangulate input"

  Every "zero-area cell" and "overlapping cells" result above came
  with NO error and NO warning. Each deck's header says what the
  original code did with it.

  HOLES. triangulate takes one ordered vertex list and has no holes.
  A polygon with holes is given as a "keyhole": each hole is joined to
  the outside by a zero-width slit, travelled both ways, so the slit's
  two end vertices are listed twice. triangulate's filter then merges
  each repeated vertex and the slit becomes an ordinary interior edge,
  used by two cells. make_polygons.py builds keyholes (keyhole()).
  This happens inside triangulate: it always ends with filter / 1,0,0,
  rmpoint / compress and resetpts / itp (in the original code too).

  The checker PROVES each hole is a real hole (Oct 12 2026), on the
  mesh's own vertex numbers: no two mesh vertices at the same place
  (duplicate_vertices 0), one closed boundary loop per outline and per
  hole (boundary_loops), and Euler number V - E + F = 1 - holes per
  polygon (0 for an annulus). A slit left doubled would join the hole
  to the outside: one loop, Euler 1, and the deck FAILS. The table line
  says "N hole(s) real: L boundary loops". Before this, vertices were
  matched by coordinates only, which could not tell two unmerged
  copies of a slit vertex from one.

  SPEED. The time is printed, not judged. Most shapes take about n^2;
  the zig-zag band takes n^3 (sandbox: 502 vertices 0.3 s, 1002 2.2 s,
  2002 18 s, 4002 145 s), because only a few ears are found on each
  pass over the polygon. That is the original algorithm, not the fix.

  THE LETTERS (tri01). Seven polygons spell LaGriT; the "a" has a hole
  (keyhole), the "i" is a stem and a dot. Each letter is triangulated
  on its own mesh object and merged into one (addmesh / merge), then:
    out_tri01_letters_tri.inp   the triangulated letters: few, long,
                                thin cells (smallest angle about 5 deg)
    out_tri01_letters_ref.inp   after two passes of refine / rivara
                                (edges longer than EDGELEN1 = 0.5,
                                then EDGELEN2 = 0.25), each followed by
                                smooth / esug + recon three times (the
                                very last recon is recon / 1), then
                                resetpts / itp. Two passes (user, Oct
                                12) because one straight to 0.25 left
                                the starting mesh's long cells visible.
  The second is checked with check_triangulate.py --refined: new
  vertices allowed, but the mesh must still fill the seven polygons
  exactly, with its boundary on theirs and every corner kept. Its
  smallest angle must be at least 22 deg ("# min_angle : 22", HARD).
  Oct 11 2026 (one pass, recon / 0): 4.8 deg after step 1 (24 of 40
  cells under 20 deg), 22.7 deg after step 2. Oct 12 (two passes,
  recon / 1): 23.9 deg, no cell under 20, largest boundary angle
  89.85 deg. The
  table line shows "min angle A -> B deg" for every deck with a
  refined check.

  CONTROL VOLUME CONDITION (tri01, tri07-09; user, Oct 12 2026). The
  meshes feed a control volume solver that uses the Voronoi cells of
  the vertices (two point flux). So no cell on the boundary may face
  the boundary with an angle over 90 deg: its circumcentre must not be
  outside the domain. The LAST recon is recon / 1, which adds vertices
  on the boundary where needed to get this ("A point-placement strategy
  for conforming Delaunay tetrahedralization", IJCGA 11(6) 669-682,
  2001; SODA 2000 pp. 67-74). "# boundary_angle : 90" makes it a HARD
  check; offending boundary edges are drawn THICK MAGENTA. With recon/0
  only (the Oct 11 run), tri01's smoothed mesh had 163 such boundary
  edges (largest facing angle 122.5 deg). The filter and rmpoint /
  compress that used to end step 2 are gone: on a correct mesh they do
  nothing; the checker fails coincident or unused vertices instead.

  THE HOLES REFINED (tri07-09, added Oct 12 2026). The same step 2
  (EDGELEN1 = 0.5 then EDGELEN2 = 0.25) on the three keyhole polygons, so the mesh around
  each hole and slit can be seen after smoothing. Oct 12 (user's Mac):
  smallest angle tri07 24.76, tri08 23.97, tri09 30.55 deg; largest
  boundary angle 89.81, 89.59, 87.99 deg; no cell under 20 deg. Limits
  set a little under: "# min_angle" 22, 22, 28.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
THE BUGS (all fixed in triangulate_lg.f, Oct 2026)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

  1. TOLERANCE IN ABSOLUTE UNITS. Collinearity was decided by
     comparing twice a triangle's area with a fixed 1e-8, whatever the
     units. On a dense boundary every vertex triple falls under it:
     a unit square with more than 10 000 vertices per side, or a
     circle of radius 1 with 2917 or more (radius 0.01: from 136),
     has NO ear left, and triangulate gives up. The thresholds were
     predicted from the formula and confirmed to the exact vertex.
     Near-touching geometry was misjudged the same way (tri28, tri29).
     Fix: one length tolerance, 1e-12 x the polygon's bounding box
     diagonal, used by every geometric test.

  2. A DIAGONAL ALONG THE BOUNDARY. The test for a valid ear diagonal
     counted "on the line" as inside, and the crossing test skipped
     every polygon edge touching the diagonal's two ends. So a
     diagonal running along a straight side, THROUGH a vertex, was
     accepted. The result is a zero-area cell, or, with the vertex
     just off the line, overlapping cells. This is GitHub issue 239
     (tri39, tri41) and the "many collinear vertices" failure (tri16
     .. tri26). Fix: a diagonal through any polygon vertex is
     rejected.

  3. TWO VERTICES NEVER TRIED AS EARS. The loop stopped one short, so
     the first and last vertex in the list could never be an ear tip.
     Fix: every vertex is tried.

  4. LARGE COORDINATES. Areas were computed as x1*y2 - y1*x2 + ...,
     which cancels away all precision far from the origin (tri33).
     Fix: computed from coordinate differences (the same value).

  5. SMALLER ONES. Fewer than 3 vertices gave no error (tri38); the
     cell count printed as **** above 9999 cells; an uninitialised
     variable was read; a wrap-around test could never be true.

  Unchanged on purpose: every cell is written CLOCKWISE, whichever
  keyword is given (seen in the real issue 239 output and in every
  sandbox run). The checker reports it (cell_orientation) but does not
  judge it.

  LIMIT OF THE FIX: features closer than about 1e-12 of the polygon's
  size cannot be told apart. Such a polygon now FAILS with "Unable to
  triangulate input"; it does not produce a bad mesh.

  SPEED: ear clipping here is O(n^2). tri21, 40 004 vertices, takes
  about 34 s, so it runs only with BIG=1. The time is printed for
  every deck but not judged.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
RESULTS
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

  PASS      expect valid: no ERROR, the mesh was written, every HARD
            check in check_triangulate.py passes, and the deck's
            "# warns :" text (if any) was printed
  REFUSED   expect error: an ERROR containing the deck's "# reason :"
            text, and no cells written
  SKIP      a "# big : yes" deck (tri21) in a run without BIG=1
  FAIL      anything else

  The exit status is nonzero on any FAIL.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
READING THE PICTURE
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

  black line    the polygon outline
  grey          cells
  RED line      a zero-area cell (three collinear vertices)
  BLACK cell    an inverted cell (orientation opposite to the rest)
  ORANGE edge   a polygon edge not used by exactly one cell
  AMBER cell    a poor cell: its smallest angle is under 20 degrees
  MAGENTA edge  a boundary edge faced by an angle over 90 degrees
                (circumcentre outside: breaks the control volume rule);
                drawn only on meshes judged for it (after recon / 1)
  DASHED BLUE   a keyhole slit: listed twice in the polygon, an
                INTERIOR edge of the mesh (not boundary)

  A deck with two meshes (tri07-09) shows them SIDE BY SIDE:
  triangulated on the left, refined and smoothed on the right. Deck
  header "# picture : large" (tri01) puts them one over the other at
  full width; "# picture : small" (tri05, tri06, tri10-41) draws at
  half width (user, Oct 12).

  Above 6000 cells only the bad cells are drawn.

